            "outcome": {
              "failure": null,
              "result": {
                "answer": "**Inflation is easing slightly in the United States, but it remains elevated, and energy is the main reason.** Globally, the picture is uneven, with renewed energy pressure interrupting earlier progress.\n\nAssuming you mean the U.S., the latest available CPI data as of September 9, 2026 are for July:\n\n| Measure | Latest reading | What it means |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Headline CPI | 3.4% year over year | Slightly cooler than June\u2019s 3.5% |\n| Core CPI | 2.5% year over year | Underlying inflation excluding food and energy is more subdued |\n| Monthly CPI | 0.1% | Prices rose only modestly during July |\n\nThe split matters. Energy prices were 14.7% higher than a year earlier, led by a 24.6% rise in gasoline. By contrast, food inflation was 3.0% and shelter inflation was 3.2%. During July itself, falling energy prices helped restrain the overall increase, while shelter accounted for most of the remaining monthly rise.\n\nOutside the U.S., inflation has recently picked up in some places. The euro area\u2019s preliminary annual rate rose to 3.3% in August from 2.9% in July. At the global level, inflation eased to 4.7% in June after reaching 4.9% in May, with the IMF attributing much of the earlier increase to renewed energy-price pressure associated with Middle East tensions.\n\n**Bottom line:** inflation is no longer accelerating broadly across most U.S. categories, but it is not fully under control. Core inflation looks calmer than the headline number, while volatile energy costs remain the biggest near-term risk.",
